• Considered ABCL’s accounting policies for estimation of Expected Credit Loss on loans and assessing compliance with the policies in terms of Ind AS 109;
• Assessed consistency of accounting policies with those applied in the previous year and obtained an understanding of the ABCL management's updated processes, systems and controls implemented in relation to impairment allowance process;
• Data inputs - The application of ECL model required multiple internal and external data inputs. This increases the risk of completeness and accuracy of the data that has been used to create assumptions in the model.
• Model estimations – Inherently judgmental models are used to estimate ECL which involves determining Probabilities of Default (“PD”), Loss Given Default (“LGD”) and Exposures at Default (“EAD”). The PD and the LGD are the key drivers of the estimation complexity in the ECL and as a result are considered the most significant judgmental aspect of the Company’s modelling approach. Management overlays were introduced on certain asset classes forward – looking risk indicators warranted additional provisioning.

• Obtained an understanding of and evaluated the design, implementation and operating effectiveness of key internal controls over the ECL computation, the modelling techniques adopted by the ABCL including application controls with respect to completeness and accuracy and report generation through interface of relevant systems, Further, performed walk through of the process involving manual control to ascertain the maker checker controls;
• Tested the completeness and accuracy of the key inputs and assumptions considered for calculation, recording, monitoring of the impairment loss recognized and staging of assets;
• Economic scenarios – Ind AS 109 requires the Company to measure ECLs on an unbiased forward – looking basis reflecting a range of future economic scenarios used and the probability weights applied to them.
• On a test check basis, assessed the critical assumptions and input data used in the estimation of Expected Credit Loss models for specific key credit risk parameters, such as the segment of loan portfolios, the movement logic between stages, Exposure at default (EAD), probability of default (PD) or loss given default (LGD);

